Exactly. And sometimes what looks like losing in one game is winning in another.

You can win by:

* Beating your opponent.
* Changing the rules.
* Changing the objective.
* Creating a new game.
* Refusing to play.
* Helping everyone win.
* Realizing you already have what you were playing for.

And here’s another layer: You can win several games with the same move.

That’s leverage.

But you can also win a battle while losing the war, or win the war at the cost of everything you wanted to protect.

That’s why identifying the game matters less than identifying what you’re actually trying to preserve, achieve, or become.

And perhaps the most interesting move of all?

Make the outcome valuable regardless of which way the game unfolds.

Not invincibility. Not guaranteed victory.

Optionality.

You don’t need every door to open when you’ve built a life with more than one way forward.

There are multiple ways to win. And you get to decide what winning means.

